  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Thu Jun 25 2026\n\nThe weak front that brought scattered storms to the area last\nnight will continue to drift slowly south toward southeast\nIllinois by this evening. Scattered showers and storms are\nexpected to continue to develop along this semi-stationary\nboundary this afternoon into this evening. Then late tonight,\nthe first of multiple rounds of showers and storms is expected\nto move into the area with strong storms capable of heavy\nrainfall. Additional rounds of storms with heavy rainfall are\nexpected through Friday with the main low pressure area and\nfrontal system pushing east Friday night. Given the semi-\nstationary boundary, weak shear along the boundary, PW values\nover 2 inches, the possibility of training cells, and these \nstorms moving over areas that had some recent heavy rains last \nweekend; believe flash flooding will be an issue over on Friday.\nModels are in good agreement on the forecast position of the\nfront being across southwest to southeast IL through the period,\nso Flood Watch location looks good. However, some guidance is\npushing some of the heavy rainfall a little more north, so have\nadded a row of counties to the current watch and kept the same\ntime period. There is a 65-75% probability of rainfall across\nthe watch area being over 1.5 inches. This is a little lower\nthan yesterday afternoon's forecast, but pockets of around 2\ninches of rainfall is still possible. \n\nNot to be forgotten, but isolated severe storms are also\npossible this afternoon through Saturday night. The main threat\nwill be damaging winds, but an isolated tornado or two is\npossible Friday afternoon and evening in southeast IL. \n\nOnce this system moves to the east, a summertime ridge will\nbuild into the midwest. Forecast for hot and humid conditions\nhas not changed from the last two days as all guidance continues\nto show dewpoints still getting into the mid 70s and highs \nreaching into the lower to middle 90s from Sunday afternoon \nthrough Wednesday. Therefore, confidence is quite high that heat\nindicies will reach above 100 over most of the area for all \nfour days.",
